# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, so without this file every
# shell script in the tree is checked out with CRLF on a Windows clone — and
# bash cannot run those. The failure is not a message about line endings: the
# script dies with **exit 127 and no output at all**, which whatever invoked it
# then reports as its own kind of failure. That cost real time here —
# sdk/tools/make-bundle.ps1 ran the vendored-header checker, got 127, and
# announced "vendored headers have drifted from the engine tree", sending the
# investigation at the headers rather than at the newline.
#
# Everything in this repo that a shell or a shebang reads must therefore be
# pinned to LF. The scripts are authored on Linux and stored LF; this only
# governs what lands in a Windows working tree.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf

# Checksum manifests. sha256sum -c reads the filename to end-of-line, so a CRLF
# checkout makes every entry name end in \r and each line fails with
# "No such file or directory" -- against files that are sitting right there.
# The repo blob is LF either way; this is about what a Windows working tree gets.
SHA256SUMS      text eol=lf

# Shebang scripts with no extension.
sdk/tools/starling-create        text eol=lf
build/session/starling-session   text eol=lf

# The .desktop, polkit policy and udev rules the session installs verbatim are
# read by Linux services; keep them LF as well.
*.desktop       text eol=lf
*.policy        text eol=lf
*.rules         text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the other direction: Windows PowerShell 5.1 fails to terminate
# a here-string in an LF-only file, and the parse error it emits points at a
# brace hundreds of lines away. sdk/tools/*.ps1 avoid here-strings for exactly
# this reason, but pinning CRLF removes the trap for anything added later.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched.
*.zip           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.deb           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.mp4           binary
*.dat           binary
